## 1. Introduction
The gradually increasing demand for clean water and renewable energy has become a top priority worldwide over the past few years [1,2]. Significantly, water and energy have always been closely linked by features of two highly related families. On the one hand, wastewater is unanimously recognized as an important source of clean water and energy. On the other hand, the treatment of wastewater also consumes a certain amount of electric energy [3]. Therefore, there is an urgent need to develop efficient and sustainable wastewater treatment technology. Microbial fuel cells (MFCs) have captured substantial attention due to its unique dual functions of harvesting electricity from organic matter and simultaneously treating wastewater [4–6]. At the anode, the anaerobic bacteria decompose organic matter into gases $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ , protons $\left(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}\right)$ , and electrons $\left(\mathbf{e}^{-}\right)$ as metastatic products, where the produced protons are transferred into the solution and the released electrons travel through external circuit to the cathode as well as combining with electron acceptor [7]. Note that the electrochemical performance of MFCs can be closely related to the following factors, such as configuration structure, anodic electrogenic bacteria, growth media, and cathodic oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) kinetics [8–10]. Among them, the sluggish kinetics of ORR have been recognized as the main impediment for the large-scale application of MFCs with molecular oxygen as electron acceptors [11]. Consequently, extensive researches have been performed in developing functional electrocatalyst to decrease the activation energy barriers and reduction overpotential, thus facilitating faster electrochemical reactions [12,13]. Up to now, Pt and its alloy are considered as the most efficient electrocatalysts for improving abovementioned sluggish electrochemical reaction, yet the practical application has been severely hampered owing to some disappointing factors including poor stability, high cost, scarcity, and unsatisfactory durability [14]. Thus, painstaking efforts are needed to design promising alternatives with high activity and better ecofriendliness for replacing $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ -based ORR catalysts.
Currently, carbon-based materials are broadly studied due to the comparatively low cost, naturally rich abundance, and comparably high physicochemical stability [15]. Generally, the electrocatalytic activity are determined by the intrinsic active sites for a given electrocatalyst. In this regard, the poor intrinsic activity of pure carbon matrix can be regulated via optimizing the structure by introducing defects, surface modification, and heteroatomdoping strategies [16–20], etc. Preferentially, non-metallic elements have been extensively introduced into carbon matrix through the pretreatment of heteroatom-enriched precursors in insert atmosphere or posttreatment of heteroatom sources with carbon matrix. Of specific interest is inducing the charge redistribution by the electronegativity difference between carbon atom and other heteroatoms [21]. Among which nitrogen (N) atom with strong electronegativity (3.04) may drag electrons away from the adjacent carbon (C) atom (2.55), leading it partially positively charged, which changes the electrical neutrality and produces available charged active sites that beneficial for oxygen adsorption during ORR process. For example, Gong et al. [22] demonstrated that N induced charge delocalization may alter $0_{2}$ chemisorption mode from the end-on to side-on adsorption mode. The parallel diatomic adsorption contributed to weaken the $_{0-0}$ bond and further facilitate the electrochemical reduction of oxygen. Therefore, the electrocatalytic activity for ORR can be effectively tailored through controlling the introduction of heteroatom. In addition, for the commonly studied candidates, metal compounds have also shown good activity toward oxygen catalysis. However, the low electronic conductivity restricts the utilization of catalytic sites owing to the semiconductor nature [23]. Keeping this in consideration, the integration concept of metal and metal compound to generate Mott-Schottky heterojunction is well proposed and considered as an effective strategy to reform the catalytic activity [24]. Especially, electrons will flow through the semiconductor/ metal interfaces spontaneously, optimizing the work function and changing surface electronic state of catalyst, which is beneficial to alter the surface properties of heterostructure and favor electrocatalysis based on the anticipated synergistic effect. For example, Liang et al. [25] fabricated a novel $\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}S_{4}$ Mott– Schottky heterojunction with surface sulfur–vacancy modification, the assembled catalyst delivered a reduced charge–discharge overpotentials when used in Li- $.0_{2}$ batteries. In another example, Yang et al. [26] demonstrated that the spontaneous electron transfer for Co to ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ ensures the upshift of $d$ -band center for ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ heterojunction, which is benefit for the intermediate adsorption during oxygen catalytic process confirmed by theoretical calculation verification. Although the heterojunction catalysts haven been accomplished for oxygen evolution reaction [27], hydrodechlorination reaction [28], and water splitting [29], such heterojunctions have been rarely reported for oxygen reduction reaction. Therefore, the hybridization of heteroatom-doped carbon coupling with heterojunction may be a feasible and promising strategy to enhance the catalytic activity.
Besides, oxygen reduction reaction is a complicated process in which various adsorption-desorption procedures may occur at the three-phase boundary. Hence, the mass transport process also plays a crucial role in determining the electrochemical performance. To date metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), an emerging subclass of crystalline materials, which assembled through the coordination of metal nodes and organic linkers, provides promising template for preparing various kinds of porous target electrocatalysts via controlled pyrolysis process [30]. Particularly, zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) as the most popular subclass of MOFs with sufficient N atoms has been chosen as considerable single-source precursor to synthesize metal/metal compounds and $N$ -doped carbon without the addition of foreign metal species or N sources [31,32]. The ZIF-67 derived target product contains high-content N with strong bond and electronegativity, which may offer high-density positive charge for the adsorption of $0_{2}$ and break of $_{0-0}$ bond [4]. However, powdered ZIF-67 particles with nanostructures at the macro level and the derived carbon matrix wrapped metal nanoparticles at the micro level usually occur in severe aggregation phenomenon, which seriously affects their dispersion and utilization efficiency, resulting in poor electrical conductivity and mass activity required for oxygen catalysis [33]. Polyacrylonitrile (PAN) is a kind of macromolecular compound, which is obtained by free radical polymerization of monomer acrylonitrile and is often used as the raw material of electrospun nanofibers. On the one hand, PAN nanofibers are one-dimensional (1D) nanomaterials with high specific surface area, porosity and aspect ratio. The derived carbon nanofibers often possess excellent mechanical properties and attractive carbon content. On the other hand, the electrospun nanofibers with appreciable structural flexibility and diversity can be considered as the supporting substrate for MOFs uniformly dispersed and highly active crystal surface exposed [34]. The carbonized interlaced nanofibers may greatly guarantee ample active sites and increase electrochemical active area. In addition, the surface coupling of heterogeneous phase and carbon nanofiber support may also generate a strong synergistic effect to improve the catalytic performance.
Herein, we design and develop a novel heterostructure composed of cobalt/cobalt phosphide and cobalt/cobalt sulfide nanoparticles embedded in $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon nanofiber (denoted as ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@}}N{\mathsf{-C N F}}$ and ${\mathsf C o}/{\mathsf C o}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N–{\mathsf C}{\mathsf N}{\mathsf F}.$ . As expected, the desired catalysts display considerably improved ORR activity close to $\sf P t/C$ . Moreover, the practical application of heterogeneous catalysts is assessed by MFCs systems, and the MFCs equipped with heterogeneous composite cathode have achieved high power density and long-term stability.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of PAN-ZIF-67
In a typical synthesis, $0.5\textrm{g C o}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $1.0{\ }\mathrm{g}$ PAN were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF with stirring for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ resulting in the electrospun solution. Then, the obtained light red solution was loaded into a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ syringe for the electrospinning process, and the corresponding optimized parameters were as follows: drum speed, working voltage, distance between the collector and syringe, flow rate are 100 rpm, $18~\mathrm{kV}$ , $15\ \mathrm{cm}$ , and $1~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. The obtained PAN-Co nanofiber was vacuum dried at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight. Cobalt salt precursor introduced into the spinning solution aims to build a coordination environment on the nanofiber surface for the in-situ growth of ZIF-67 particles. Briefly, one piece of fibrous membrane was first added to the $10~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol $\mathrm{\langleCH_{3}O H\rangle}$ solution of 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIM, $0.93~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then another $5~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(0.33\mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}}.$ was subsequently dropped into above system for another $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ After that, the purple membrane (PAN-ZIF-67) was washed with methanol and finally dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2. Synthesis of Co@N-CNF, Co/CoP/Co2P@N-CNF, and $C o/C o S_{2}@N-$ CNF
## 2.3. Materials characterization
The morphologies of the as-synthesized samples were characterized by field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, SUPAR 55, Germany) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2200FS, Japan). The phase compositions were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD, SMATLAB (3), Japan) with $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{Q}$ $(\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Bruker spectrometer with a $532~\mathrm{nm}$ laser source was employed to record Raman spectra (HR800, France). The pyrolysis characteristic of ZIF-67 was carried out on a thermogravimetric analysis instrument (TGA, TA, USA). Surface element compositions and electronic states were analyzed by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250XI, USA).
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
Electrocatalytic performance were recorded by cyclic voltammetry (CV), linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) on a rotating disc electrode (AUTOLAB, RDE). The electrochemical measurements were conducted in 0.1 M KOH solution saturated with $0_{2}$ . A standard three electrode cell was assembled by a glassy carbon disk (area: $0.19\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) coated with prepared catalysts, $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode, and Pt electrode as working, reference, and counter electrode, respectively. Before the experiment, the glassy carbon electrode was polished with ${\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ power $(0.05~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . The catalyst ink was prepared by mixing catalyst $(2{\mathrm{~mg}})$ , $5\%$ Nafion solution $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(100\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and isopropanol $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ via ultrasonic water bath treatment. Subsequently, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ink was cast onto the cleaned glassy carbon electrode surface and dried at room temperature. The CV test was performed at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ and LSV measurement was conducted at different rotation rates ranging from 400 to $2045~\mathrm{rpm}$ . EIS characterization was carried out at open circuit voltage by scanning the frequency range from 0.1 to $100000\ \mathrm{Hz}$ The electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ was obtained to assess the electrochemically active area in a non-faradaic region (0.97 to 1.07 V) with scan rate ranging from 20 to $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . Methanol tolerance was performed in 0.1 M KOH and 1 M $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ solution.
