## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural characterization of the electrocatalysts
Taking into account the merits of electrospinning nanofibers and ZIF derivatives, the $\mathsf{C o@{N-C N F}}$ intermediate product is fabricated through an in-situ growth strategy and pyrolysis treatment of PAN-ZIF-67 precursor. As shown in Fig. 1a, the PAN-Co nanofibers are first prepared by electrospinning strategy. The field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) image identifies the regular 1D structure of PAN-Co nanofibers with smooth surface (Fig. S1). When PAN-Co nanofibers are first immersed into 2- MeIM $\mathrm{\vertCH_{3}O H}$ solution via in-situ primary growth, the visible color transformation from light red to lilac appears on nanofiber membrane. Obviously, the ZIF-67 nanoparticles are sparsely loaded on the surface of nanofiber, which is unable to cover fiber surface tightly due to the insufficient coordination between metal ions and organic ligands (Fig. S2). With the aim of complete coverage, another $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{O H}$ solution is then added to the above system for an in-situ secondary growth, forming the uniform ‘‘coreshell” structure with ‘‘ZIF-67” shell well-tiled on ‘‘PAN” core (Fig. 2a). The highly stable core-shell nanofibers are further converted to porous carbon-based nanofiber $(\mathsf{C o@{N-C N F}})$ by in-situ pyrolysis treatment in air and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, respectively. SEM image presents a crumpled surface with decreased nanofiber diameter, which may be attributed to the evaporation of volatile species associated with the collapse of ZIF-67 skeleton (Fig. 2b). The surface microstructure morphologies of Co@N-CNF are further observed utilizing transmission electron microscopy (TEM) technique. It is clear that numerous black nanoparticles are uniformly dispersed in the internal and external structure of nanofibers (Fig. 2c). The crumpled surface is obviously discerned, which may provide large specific surface area and ample accessible active sites. The representative high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images of nanoparticles show that the well-exposed lattice fringe spacing of $0.205~\mathrm{{nm}}$ resulted from metal Co nanoparticles. In addition, Co nanoparticles are encapsulated by graphite shell with interlayer spacing of $0.35\ \mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to (002) plane of graphite (Fig. 2d), which is formed due to the catalytic effect of Co species [36]. Furthermore, the exposed Co nanoparticles may provide more anchoring sites for the formation of heterojunction structures.
Fig. 1b and 1c present the illustrative scheme of in-situ phosphorization and sulfurization strategies to synthesize ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}-$ $\mathsf{P}@N\mathrm{-CNF}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@N.$ -CNF heterostructures. Typically, the $\mathsf{C o@}N.$ -CNF intermediates are thermally-treated in the presence of $\Nu\mathrm{aH_{2}P O_{2}}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and sulfur powders with flowing Ar. During this process, the generated $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ from $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and sublimated sulfur flow along the upstream to downstream and combine with Co resource to form metal compound. As showed in Fig. 2e and 2f, the ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o P}/{\sf C o_{2}P@{N}}$ -CNF composite reveal an uneven morphology attached to the surface of nanofibers, which may be attributed to the sustained reaction of phosphorus source and $\mathsf{C o@}N.$ -CNF. TEM image in Fig. $2\mathrm{g}$ illustrates the encapsulation structure of nanoparticles in the derived carbon nanofibers, which is conductive to offer good electronic conductivity for the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N-$ CNF hybrid. The HRTEM image in Fig. 2h and 2i reveal the crystalline structure of adjacent compound nanoparticles, with apparent lattice spacings of 0.189 and $0.325~\mathrm{{nm}}$ assignable to the (211) of CoP plane and (020) plane of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ respectively. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern in Fig. 2j reveals that the periodic arrangement of two-dimensional diffraction points, indicating the single-crystal nature. Elemental analysis is also performed to demonstrate the composition and distribution of ${\mathsf C}_{}{\mathsf{o}}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}@N.$ -CNF. EDX elemental mapping and line scan profiles demonstrate the evenly distribution of C, N, Co and $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ elements over the entire carbon nanofibers (Fig. 2k and 2l). In addition, SEM and TEM images of ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}\mathsf S_{2}@N–{\mathsf C}_{\mathrm{NF}}$ are displayed in Fig. 2- $\mathrm{m}{-}2\mathrm{t}.$ . It can be seen from the SEM images that numerable in-situ formed particles with a small size appear on the epitaxial surface of nanofibers, which may belong to the precipitation effect at high temperature (Fig. 2m and 2n). Fig. 2o and 2p present the TEM images of ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N$ -CNF, and it is observed that the generated homogeneous nanoparticles are well-confined within the carbon nanofibers framework integrated, in agreement with the SEM observation. The HRTEM image in Fig. 2q displays obvious interplanar spacing of 0.229 and $0.278{\ \mathrm{nm}}$ between the adjacent lattice fringes correspond to the (211) and (200) facets of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , respectively. The SAED pattern in Fig. 2r indicates that the major bright diffraction rings match well with the crystalline $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles. As shown in EDX mappings and line scan profiles, C, N, Co and S elements are also evenly distributed on the surface of ${\sf C o}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF, indicating the successful coverage of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles (Fig. 2s and 2t). It is worth nothing that there are no corresponding lattice fringes of metallic cobalt for the as-prepared samples, which may be attributed to the preferential reaction of surface cobalt particles. Therefore, the unreacted Co nanoparticles are mainly distributed under the compound shell. In summary, these results indicate that the combination of metal, metal compound, and N-doped carbon nanofibers may possess a synergistic effect for the ORR progress.
The phase structure of prepared heterogeneous electrocatalysts is determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis. As depicted in Fig. S3, the characteristic diffraction peaks of the prepared PANZIF-67 are well matched with previous reports, confirming the successful synthesis [37,38]. After pre-oxidation in $0_{2}$ atmosphere, the characteristic peaks of ZIF-67 are well reserved (Fig. S4), indicating the high structural stability. Then, only diffraction peaks correspond to metallic Co are found after annealing at argon atmosphere (Fig. 3a). A couple of peaks centered at $44.2\ensuremath{^\circ}$ and $51.5^{\circ}$ correspond to the (111) and (200) planes of Co (PDF #15-0806). Moreover, a broad and weak peak located at about $26.0^{\circ}$ belongs to the (002) facet of graphitic carbon (PDF #41-1487). The formed Co nanoparticles and graphitic carbon are derived from the reduction of cobalt ions, organic ligand and PAN substrate during the pyrolysis process. After subsequent low-temperature phosphorization reaction in argon atmosphere, the $C_{0}@N.$ -CNF composites are converted into $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P@}N\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CN}$ F. The XRD pattern shows several main peaks at $31.6^{\circ}$ , $46.2^{\circ}$ , and $48.1^{\circ}\mathrm{~.~}$ , corresponding to the (011), (112), and (211) facets of CoP (PDF #29-0497), respectively. Besides, another set of strong peaks centered at $40.7^{\circ}$ , $40.9^{\circ}$ , and $43.3^{\circ}$ can be assigned to the (121), (201), and (211) facets of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ (PDF #32-0306), indicating the heterogeneous composition of cobalt phosphide species. In the case of ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N$ -CNF sample, several preferential peaks at $32.3^{\circ}$ , $36.2^{\circ}$ , and $54.9^{\circ}$ are indexed to the (200), (210), and (311) facets of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ (PDF #41- 1471), respectively. In addition to partial metallic Co nanoparticles are well reserved without being consumed, the metal compounds are also obtained, which integrates multiple electrocatalytic sites (Co, $\mathsf C0_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf P/\mathsf C0\mathsf S_{2}$ , and N-CNF) for ORR activity simultaneously.
Raman spectra are further conducted to evaluate the graphitization degree of carbon nanofibers in the prepared materials. As shown in Fig. 3b, typical D-band at $\sim1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and G-band at $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are obviously observed, which represent the disorder and crystallinity of carbon nanofibers, respectively [39]. The corresponding intensity ratios of $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ are calculated to be 1.01 $\mathsf{T o@{N-}}$ CNF), 1.11 $(\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C o P}/\mathsf{C o_{2}P@N\mathrm{-}C N F})$ , and 1.16 $(\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N–\mathsf{C N F})$ , respectively. It means that both phosphorization and sulfurization treatment may introduce more defects into carbon matrix. The generated defective carbon has been reported to be significant catalytic sites by tailoring the electronic structure of electrocatalyst [40,41]. Moreover, several bands at 467.7, 507.7, and $673.1~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}},$ , $\mathrm{F}_{2\mathrm{g}},$ and $\mathsf{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}$ vibration modes of metallic Co or Co-based compound [42]. Fig. 3c shows the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curve of PAN-ZIF-67 annealed under argon atmosphere. It can be seen that the sample mainly presents weight loss in three stages. First, the rapid weight loss of $16.34\%$ from $20^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $230^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ can be attributed to the cyclization and dehydrogenation reactions of PAN nanofibers [43]. Second, the event accompanying $22.84\ \%$ weight loss up to $397^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ can be due to the persistent decomposition of PAN followed the conversion into carbon matrix. As the pyrolysis temperature increases to $800~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the final mass loss ratio of $33.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ could be ascribed to the decomposition of ZIF-67 with the char yield about $27\%$ [33]. Therefore, the eventual annealing temperature under argon atmosphere is designated at $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to completely convert PAN-ZIF-67.
The elemental composition and valence state of the as-prepared electrocatalysts are further analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra. As shown in Fig. S5, the survey spectra confirm the coexistence of N, C, Co, O, P and S elements in the desired samples. The specific element content is listed in Table S1, among which the content of C and N for $\mathsf{C o@}N$ -CNF are higher than ${\mathsf C}_{}{\mathsf{o}}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}@N-$ CNF and ${\mathsf C o}/{\mathsf C o}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N{\mathsf{-C N F}}$ , which may be due to the phosphorization and sulfurization reactions on nanofiber surface. Fig. 3d presents three forms of Co species for the fitted Co 2p spectrum of Co@N-CNF. The characteristic peaks located at 778.6 and 793.7 eV demonstrate the zerovalent state of cobalt $(\mathsf{C o}^{0})$ [44]. Two peaks at 780.2 and $795.7\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the trivalent cobalt ion $(\mathsf{C o}^{3+})$ , while the other two peaks centered at 780.3 and $795.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the divalent cobalt ion $(\mathsf{C o}^{2+})$ with corresponding satellite peaks at 786.8 and $803.9{\mathrm{~eV}}$ [41]. It is noticed that ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ are also detected besides ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ , which may be due to the surface oxidation at air atmosphere and the surface sensitive characterization of XPS $\cdot\mathord{\sim}10\mathrm{nm})$ . In addition to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ , another doublet peaks can be resolved at 781.6 $\mathtt{(C o2p_{3/2})}$ and $797.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Co $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ ) accompanied by a couple of satellite peaks for ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}.$ P@N-CNF and ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF composites, which is related to the formation of Co-P or Co-S bond, consistent with ${\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathrm{{x}}}{\mathsf{P}}$ and $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ species [45,46]. Notably, the binding energy of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ in $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}@N$ -CNF presents a positive shift (778.8 and $793.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ than that of $\mathsf{C o@{N-}}$ CNF and ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o P}/{\sf C o}_{2}{\sf P@}N.$ -CNF (778.6 and $793.7\ \mathrm{eV};$ , indicating lower electron cloud density. It has been reported that the values of work function for metallic Co and $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ are about $5.0~\mathrm{eV}$ and $5.5\ \mathrm{{\eV},}$ respectively, meeting the requirement of constructing Schottky barrier [26,47]. When the Schottky contact between Co and $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ is formed, the electrons will spontaneously transfer from Co to $\phantom{}C0S_{2}$ across the heterogeneous interface until the equivalent work function is achieved. Consequently, holes will be left on metallic Co side and electrons can be enriched on the side of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , which can promote processive electrons flow by the formed space-charge region. The electrophilic area of Co side is conductive to stabilize the adsorbed $0_{2}$ and improve the ORR performance [26]. In the high-resolution P 2p spectrum (Fig. 3e), three main peaks centered at 129.5, 130.4 and $134.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ were detected, which are attributed to the $\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , P $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathsf{P}{-}0$ for ${\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathbf{x}}{\mathsf{P}}$ [48]. Moreover, the S 2p high-resolution spectrum in Fig. 3f can be deconvoluted into three peaks at 163.2 $({\textsf{P}}2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2})$ , 164.5 $(\mathsf{P}\ 2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and 168.8 eV (SAO), respectively, confirming the successful synthesis of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ [49]. The electrocatalytic performance can be influenced by the configuration of N doping and atomic percentage of various N species. In Fig. 3g, the high-resolution N 1s spectra demonstrate the presence of three types of N species composed of pyridinic-N $(\sim398.9\$ eV), pyrrolic-N ( $\sim399.9$ eV), and graphitic-N $(\sim401.4\mathrm{~~}\mathrm{eV})$ , revealing the $\mathsf{N}$ doping strategy. Specifically, pyridinic- and pyrrolic-N with a planar configuration combine with two carbon atoms in the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybrid orbital, while graphitic-N is bonded with three carbon atoms in the graphitized carbon [50]. In general, pyridinic-N is conducive to improve the surface wettability and onset potential, and graphitic-N plays a critical role in enhancing the limiting current density as well as improve the structural stability of carbon matrix [51–53]. As shown in Fig. 3h and Table S2, more pyridinic-N $(44.46~\%)$ exists for $\mathsf{C o@{N-C N F}}$ which is higher than that of ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{CoP}}/{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P@}}N{\mathrm{-C}}.$ NF $(27.83\ \%)$ and ${\mathsf C o}/{\mathsf C o}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N{\mathsf{-C N F}}$ $(23.44\ \%)$ . Remarkably, ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{CoP}}/{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}@N{\mathrm{-CN}}$ F and C ${}_{0}/C0S_{2}@N$ -CNF possess the high percentage content of graphiticN, which is believed to be beneficial for ORR activity. On the one hand, $N\cdot$ -doping can not only change the spin or charge, but also open the band gap of carbon atom by lowing the valence band top and raising the conduction band bottom, further facilitating oxygen adsorption and weakening the bonding energy of oxygen [54]. In addition, the C 1s XPS spectra are presented in Fig. S6, revealing the typical characteristics of heteroatom-doped carbon matrix, in which the peaks situated at about 284.6, 285.6 and $288.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}},{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{N}}$ , and $scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}-0$ bond, respectively [55].
## 3.2. ORR performances
To assess the catalytic activity of as-prepared electrocatalysts, cyclic voltammetry (CV) and linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) are performed in $0_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution. All potentials are calibrated to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). The $\mathsf{C o@{N-}}$ CNF and $\sf P t/C$ electrocatalysts are also employed as the control and benchmark samples. As depicted in Fig. 4a, the well-defined cathodic peak can be obviously observed in the CV curve, demonstrating the apparent ORR catalytic capacities. Both $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C o P}/\mathsf{C o}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{P}@N–\mathsf{C N F}$ and ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}\mathsf C_{2}@N–{\mathsf C}_{\mathrm{NF}}$ present strong oxygen reduction peaks closer to that of $\sf P t/C$ electrode. The ORR electrocatalytic performance are further performed by LSV curves recorded on a rotating disc electrode (RDE). Generally, the onset potential (Eonset) and half-wave potential $\left(\operatorname{E}_{1/2}\right)$ are the most important performance indicators of ORR catalyst. The $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}$ can be defined as the potential corresponding to half of the limit diffusion current. As for the Eonset, the horizontal line and the falling slope are often used as tangent lines to intersect at a point, and the corresponding potential at this point is the $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{onset}},$ as shown in Fig. S7. In Fig. 4b and 4c, Co@N-CNF, $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C o P}/\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{P}\varpi N-$ CNF and ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@N$ -CNF has a similar Eonset $(\sim0.84\lor)$ , the high $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ of $\mathsf{C o@}N$ -CNF can be attributed to the high pyridinic-N content. It is worth nothing that $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CN}$ F and ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o S}_{2}@N$ -CNF exhibit the closest half-wave potential to $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ $\mathsf C\left(\mathsf E_{1/2},0.69,0.74\mathsf V\right)$ , much higher than Co@N-CNF (0.67 V). Though the Eonset is slightly lower than that of $\sf P t/C$ , the desired sample present outstanding $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ (0.74 V) and comparative diffusion limited current density $\mathrm{(J_{L})}$ of approximately $4.48\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ The calculated Tafel slop are also shown in Fig. 4d, which displays a low value ranging from 101.8 to $115.4~\mathrm{\mV~dec}^{-1}$ except for $\mathsf{C o@}N.$ -CNF, suggesting the fast kinetic ORR process with low mass and electron transfer barriers. It is worth nothing that ${\mathsf C o}/{\mathsf C o}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N-$ CNF sample demonstrates the lowest value, which may endow excellent electrochemical performance in MFC. The specific electrocatalytic performance of different catalysts in alkaline solution are shown in Table S3.
These findings indicate that ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@}}N{\mathsf{-C N F}}$ and ${\sf C o}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF possess a superior ORR electrocatalytic performance. In addition, the Koutecky-Levich (K-L) plots of $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf C,\mathsf C\mathsf{o}/\mathsf C\mathsf{o P}/\mathsf C\mathsf{o}_{2}\mathsf{-}$ $\mathsf{P}@N$ -CNF and ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N$ -CNF catalysts in the potential range show a good linearity, and the diffusion current density increases rapidly with the increase of rotation rate from 400 to 2025 rpm, indicating the reaction order of ORR follows the first-order reaction kinetics (Fig. 4e and S8). The experimental average electron transfer number (n, average value of various potentials) is about 4.0, which is higher than that of $\mathsf{C o@}N.$ -CNF sample $(\sim2.0)$ by referring to Eq. S1 and S2. Therefore, it is suggested that the ORR process main proceeds via a $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transfer pathway to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ except for $\mathsf{C o@}N.$ -CNF via a $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ transfer pathway to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Several studies have shown that N functional species possess different functions for ORR process. On the one hand, Pyridinic-N may promote the chemical adsorption of oxygen by polarizing the adjacent carbon atoms and improve the $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ for ORR [56]. On the other hand, Pyrrolic-N can enhance the adsorption and reduction of $0_{2}$ by activate the adjacent carbon atoms with electron drawing effect [57]. In addition, the graphitic-N is conductive to improve the conductivity of electrocatalysts and control the limiting current density [58,59]. The more pyridinic-N content of Co@N-CNF electrode leads to a more positive $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{onset}},$ in agreement with the experimental results.
To further comprehend the superior ORR performances of the as-prepared samples, electrochemically active surface area (ECSA), related to the double-layer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ of electrode is also calculated. Fig. S9 presents the cyclic voltammograms of electrocatalysts at various scan rates, and $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ is estimated by the linear relationship of voltammetric current against various scan rates (Fig. 4f). The calculated $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P@}N\mathrm{-CNF}$ and ${\mathsf C}_{}{\mathsf{o}}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF are confirmed to be 4.3 and $5.6~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ , which is much higher than that of $\mathsf{C o@{N-C N F}}$ $(2.6~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ ) and $\sf P t/C$ (0.5 mF $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). It means that the ORR electrocatalysis process can be greatly promoted benefit from the high surface accessibility. Then, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is subsequently employed to reveal the resistance of electrocatalysts. As shown in Fig. S10, a relevant equivalent circuit is given, where $\mathtt{R}_{\mathrm{ct}},\mathtt{R}_{\mathrm{ohm}}$ , W, and CPE represent the charge transfer resistance, ohmic resistance, Warburg impedance, and double-layer capacitance, respectively [60,61]. For the Nyquist plot, the depressed semicircle in low frequency and intercept with $\mathbf{x}$ -axis in high frequency are attributed to $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ and $\mathrm{R_{ohm}}$ . In addition, the straight long line in low frequency symbolizes the Warburg impedance. The appropriate fitting of impedance data to the equivalent circuit indicates that the $\mathsf{C o@{N-}}$ CNF sample exhibit the maximum $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ and interface diffusion resistance. For $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CN}$ F and ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@N.$ -CNF, the existence of heterogeneous phase may arouse a significant drop in $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}},$ demonstrating the higher electron transfer efficiency.
The methanol crossover effect is key criteria for the assessment of electrocatalysts stability and selectivity in membrane fuel cells. In general, methanol crossover refers to the diffusion of methanol from the anode to the cathode through the exchange membrane, which occupies the cathode catalytic site and leads to catalyst poisoning and deactivation in the reaction process, reducing the efficiency of the fuel cell. Then, LSV, CV, and EIS measurements are performed in $0_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH solution with and without methanol. As shown in Fig. $4g$ and $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the addition of methanol leads to significantly different behaviors in RDE measurements. In the presence of methanol, the $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ and limited current density suffer serious degradation with $0.20{\mathrm{V}}$ and $1.52\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ reduction compared to the curve without $1\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol. However, the ${\mathsf C}_{0}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF sample only shows little influence on limited current density, and the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N.$ -CNF electrode almost coincides with the original curve (Fig. S11). Furthermore, the obvious evidence in Fig. S12 also confirms the conversion of the dominated ORR to methanol oxidation reaction for $\sf P t/C$ electrode after methanol injection while the transition metal-based catalysts have little change. In addition, the resistance value of $\sf P t/C$ electrode increases sharply after immersion in methanol (Fig. S13), demonstrating the serious polarization phenomenon. The corresponding schematic of methanol tolerance is shown in Fig. 4i. Generally, methanol has a strong adsorption effect on the catalytic site of Pt. When the oxygen reduction reaction occurs, Pt electrode simultaneously catalyzes the highly active oxidation of methanol, thus forming methanol cross effect. However, non-precious metal catalysts usually possess robust anti-methanol property. Therefore, above results clearly indicate that the ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf P}/{\mathsf C}_{0_{2}}{\mathsf P}\ @N–{\mathsf C}{\mathsf N}$ F and ${\sf C o}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF electrocatalysts possess superior stability and good methanol tolerance compared to $\sf P t/C$ , simultaneously asserting its practical viability in direct methanol fuel cells.
## 3.3. MFCs performances
After investigating the dynamic mechanism in a three-electrode system, the prepared electrocatalysts are further employed as airbreathing electrode and evaluated in MFCs, named as ${\mathsf{M F C-P t}}/{\mathsf{C}}$ ， MFC-Co@N-CNF, MFC-Co/CoP/Co2P@N-CNF, and MFC-Co $/{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@N-$ CNF, respectively. When the output voltage of the MFCs reaches the maximum value and remains stable, the electrochemical performance of different catalyst based MFCs are compared. The specific electrocatalytic performance of MFCs decorated with different catalysts are shown in Table S4. In order to evaluate the catalytic activity of electrocatalyst in the MFCs configuration, CV and
EIS measurements are first conducted on an electrochemical workstation. It can be clearly seen from Fig. 5a that $\mathsf{M F C-C o}/\mathsf{C o P}/$ $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{P}@N$ -CNF and $\mathsf{M F C-C o/C o S}_{2}@N\mathrm{-CN}]$ F exhibit obvious redox peaks with the voltage ranging from $-0.8–0.4\ V$ vs SCE, revealing the prepared catalysts have shown superior oxygen catalytic activity. The indicative factors of charge transport and conductivity properties are then studied by EIS curves. As depicted in Fig. S14, the ohmic resistances are $2.79\Omega,3.20\Omega,2.98\Omega$ and $4.17\ \Omega$ , respectively, indicating a comparative value. In addition, the charge transfer resistances present a trend in the following order: MFC-Co@N-C $\mathrm{NF}~>~\mathrm{MFC-Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N\mathrm{-CNF}~\approx~\mathrm{MFC-Co}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\varpi N\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{CNF}>\mathrm{MFC-Pt}/\mathrm{C}.$ The lower charge transfer resistance is conducive to produce more power with higher electron transfer efficiency benefit from the loss decline on the interface of electrode and solution. The low resistances can not only improve the surface conductivity, but also enhance the charge transfer rate, eventually optimizing the ORR activity in MFCs cathode.
To evaluate the practical electrochemical performance, the polarization curves and output power are examined with an external resistance of $1000\Omega$ under the continuously thermostatic cultivation condition, in which the dependence of power density and output voltage versus current density is revealed. The corresponding results are concluded by referring to Eq. S3 and reflected in Fig. 5b. To begin with, the open circuit voltage of MFC-Co@N-CNF decreases more quickly with the increase of current density, revealing the poor charge transfer rate. It is noticed that the ${\mathrm{MFC-Pt}}/{\mathrm{C}}$ shows the higher open circuit voltage, while the voltage drops faster than that of $\mathsf{M F C-C o}/{C_{0}\mathsf{P}}/{\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{P}\mathscr{@}N\mathrm{-CNF}}$ and ${\sf M F C-C O}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF, indicating the higher stability of latter. Obviously, MFC-Co $\mathsf{\Gamma}{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}@N.$ -CNF exhibits the optimal power density (400.06 $\mathrm{mW\cm}^{-2\cdot}$ , followed by MFC-Co/CoP/ $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{P}@N$ -CNF ( $375.16\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mw}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , MFC-Pt/C $\cdot333.70\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ), and MFC-Co@N-CNF (228.98 mW $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ ). Therefore, the results indicate that MFC-Co/CoP/ $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{P}@N.$ -CNF and $\mathsf{M F C-C o/C o S_{2}@{N\mathrm{-}C N F}}$ possess higher electricity generation performance than the other two MFCs. In addition, the obtained power density of MFC modified with ${\mathsf C o}/{\mathsf C o}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N-$ CNF cathode is also superior than those of some representative cathode electrocatalysts (Table S5). The outstanding performances can be mainly attributed to the optimized N configuration as well as multiphase structure as verified by XRD and XPS measurements, and hence resulting in the superior ORR activity.
To further explore the efficiency of wastewater treatment and electronic collection, chemical oxygen demand (COD) removal and corresponding coulombic efficiency (CE) are calculated according to Eq. S4 and S5. Fig. 5c shows the average influent/effluent and COD removal rates of different MFCs in several working cycles. Results indicate that the average COD removal rates of ${\sf M F C-C O}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P}}/{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}@N.$ -CNF and $\mathrm{MFC-Co/CoS}_{2}@N-($ CNF are respectively $72.0\ \%$ and $74.1\ \mathrm{~\%~}$ which is very closer to that of MFC-Pt/C $(74.9\%)$ and much higher than MFC-Co@N-CNF $(55.7\ \%)$ . The high COD removal rate demonstrates the superior ORR efficiency which occurs at the cathode and the resulting high microbial utilization at the anode [62]. The low COD removal rate of MFC-Co@N-CNF is consistent with the poor voltage output, which can be attributed to the poor charge transfer and utilization rate at the reaction interface. In addition, coulomb efficiency (CE) symbolizes the capacity of MFCs to convert chemical energy stored in organic matter into electrical energy with the aid of electrocatalyst and microorganisms. Therefore, the CE is related to the durable activity of catalysts in the cathode and microbial activity in the anode. Normally, the catalytic activity of cathode can directly influence the CE value. The high value of CE is acquired by $\mathsf{M F C-C o/C o S}_{2}@N\mathrm{-CNF}$ $(12.4\ \%)$ , followed by $\mathrm{MFC-Co/CoP/Co_{2}P\mathcal{O}\it{N}.}$ CNF (11.2), both of which are higher than that of MFC-Pt/C $(10.4\ \%)$ and $\operatorname{MFC-CO@}N\operatorname{-}$ CNF $(6.21\ \%)$ , indicating the superior ORR kinetics to promote high efficiency of electrons recycling from the anode to cathode. Thus, the high COD removal rate and CE guarantee the stable and efficient power output for heterostructure-based MFCs.
The long-term stability tests of MFCs are further conducted to assess the cathode activity, as shown in Fig. 5d. It can be seen that all the MFCs exhibit periodic voltage generations, including initial start, stable and decline stage for each cycle. In the first several cycles, all MFCs present high voltage output, while the ${\sf M F C-C O}/$ ${\mathrm{CoP}}/{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}@N.$ -CNF and ${\mathsf{M F C-P t}}/{\mathsf{C}}$ have a significant decline in the subsequent operations, indicating the low durability. For the $\mathsf{M F C-C o/C o S}_{2}@N\mathrm{-CNF}$ , the output voltages remain stable and high that the other three MFCs (Fig. 5d and S15). The reproducible high voltage output manifests the good stability of ${\mathsf C}_{0}/{\mathsf C}_{0}{\mathsf S}_{2}@N.$ -CNF in MFCs operation. The high and stable voltage platform demonstrate the faster consumption of nutrient substance, which further proves that the ORR occurs on heterojunction cathode is more efficient than other electrodes [63]. Therefore, MFCs with promising heterojunction catalyst prepared can be integrated into series circuit to meet specific application need, as presented by Fig. 5e that shows series-connected MFCs being employed to power a temperature and humidity sensor device with superior operation stability under the condition of abundant nutrients.
These results indicate that ZIF-derived heterogeneous catalyst is a prospective alternative for efficient electricity production with long-term stability and high power density. The synergistic catalytic mechanism for ORR is presented in Fig. 5f. The superior performance can be attributed to several unique advantages of heterogeneous catalyst. First, the 1D nanofiber structure ensures uniform dispersion of ZIF-67 and prevents the metal/compound nanoparticles agglomeration and collapse of carbon framework during the high-temperature pyrolysis process, contributing to provide ample active sites and strengthen the inherent interaction between carbon nanofibers and functional nanoparticles. Second, the electrocatalytic performance is related to corresponding N doping configuration and content. Herein, the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoP}/\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\varpi N.$ CNF and ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o S}_{2}@N\$ -CNF possess optimized N configuration, which is conductive to change the spin and band gap of carbon atom, further decreasing thermodynamic barrier of ORR. Finally, based on the heterojunction effect, the electrons can be transmitted at the contact interfaces because of the differential work functions, which provides a rapid electron transfer pathway. Integrating all these desired features, the prepared heterojunction catalyst will be promising ORR catalyst in MFCs.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a novel heterostructure composed of metal cobalt and cobalt compound nanoparticles encapsulated into $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon nanofibers is successfully prepared and applied in MFCs systems. The heterogeneous catalysts achieve superior ORR catalytic activity and methanol tolerance. The enhanced electrocatalytic performance can be attributed to the synergistic effect of 1D porous structure, optimized N configuration, as well as the coupling interface regulation. Moreover, the MFCs modified with ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o}/$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@N$ -CNF demonstrates the maximum power density of $400.06\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ and coulombic efficiency of $12.4\%$ . In addition, ${\sf C o}/{\sf C o S}_{2}@N$ -CNF based MFCs shows reproducible high voltage output with good stability of operation. This work provides a promising strategy to design high-performance heterogeneous catalytic materials from the perspective of fast mass transport and electron transfer in MFCs and other membrane fuel cells. Moreover, further exploration should be carried out, such as the structure-activity relationship between electrochemical performances and components, morphology, structure, etc.
